<section class="page-hero"><img class="bg" src="/assets/img/photos/hail-shingles.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/hail-shingles-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/hail-shingles.webp 1024w" sizes="100vw" width="1024" height="683" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.12"><div class="wrap"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><span>›</span><a href="/appraisal-solutions/">Services</a><span>›</span>Hail &amp; Roof Damage Appraisal</nav><span class="kicker light up">Appraisal service</span><h1 class="up" style="animation-delay:.06s">Hail Damage Appraisal in Wisconsin</h1><p class="lede up" style="animation-delay:.14s">Wisconsin’s hail season runs roughly from late spring through summer, and storms tracking across the south and west of the state can leave whole neighborhoods with bruised shingles. The argument usually isn’t whether it hailed. It’s how much of the roof has to go.</p><div class="btn-row up" style="animation-delay:.22s"><a class="btn gold" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="24" height="24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn ghost-l" href="/contact-us/">Free claim review</a></div><div class="facts up" style="animation-delay:.3s"><div><small>Peak season</small><b>Late spring to summer</b></div><div><small>Common gap</small><b>Repair vs. replace</b></div><div><small>We document</small><b>Hits, matching, code</b></div></div></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose">
<p>Hail damage appraisal in Wisconsin usually comes down to a roof the insurer wants to patch and a homeowner or building owner who believes it needs replacing. We serve as your appraiser in that dispute, documenting the damage independently and pricing the scope your policy supports.</p>
<h2>What hail does to a roof</h2>
<p>On asphalt shingles, hail fractures the mat and knocks granules loose, leaving soft spots that age faster and can leak years later. On metal, it dents panels and can crack coatings. On cedar shakes, it splits wood along the grain. Gutters, downspouts, window wraps, siding and air conditioner fins often carry the clearest evidence of the storm’s size and direction.</p>
<h3>Functional versus cosmetic</h3>
<p>Some policies, especially for metal roofs, now exclude “cosmetic” hail damage. Whether a dent affects the roof’s function is a fact question the panel can look at, but whether cosmetic damage is covered at all is a coverage question. We flag it early so you know which conversation you are in.</p>
<h2>The usual points of disagreement</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Test square counts.</strong> How many hits per square on each slope, and whether the carrier inspected every slope.</li>
<li><strong>Repairability.</strong> Whether brittle or discontinued shingles can be repaired without breaking surrounding tabs.</li>
<li><strong>Matching.</strong> Whether new shingles or siding can reasonably match what remains.</li>
<li><strong>Code items.</strong> Ice-and-water shield at eaves, drip edge, ventilation and tear-off requirements when a roof has two layers.</li>
<li><strong>Accessories.</strong> Vents, flashing, skylights, satellite dishes and gutter guards that are easy to leave off.</li>
</ul>
<h2>How we approach a hail appraisal</h2>
<ol>
<li>We get on the roof, mark and photograph hits on every slope, and record shingle type, age and condition.</li>
<li>We check soft metals and siding to confirm storm size and direction.</li>
<li>Where repairability is in question, we document brittleness and look for discontinued product.</li>
<li>We price the scope at current local rates, including tear-off, disposal, code items and steep or high charges where they apply.</li>
<li>We compare our estimate to the carrier’s and narrow the differences with the other appraiser.</li>
</ol>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Watch the clock</b>Many policies set a deadline to report a loss or file suit, and some hail endorsements limit claims to a set period after the storm. Don’t wait for the next storm to deal with the last one.</div>
<h2>Deductibles and depreciation</h2>
<p>Some Wisconsin policies carry a separate wind and hail deductible expressed as a percentage of the dwelling limit, which can be much larger than a flat deductible. Roofs are also frequently paid at actual cash value up front, with depreciation released after replacement, and some newer policies pay roofs at actual cash value only. Appraisal doesn’t change those terms, but the award should apply them correctly. Our <a href="/claims-dictionary/">claims dictionary</a> explains the jargon.</p>
<p>For storms that brought both hail and damaging wind, see our <a href="/appraisal-solutions/tornado-straight-line-wind-appraisal/">wind damage appraisal</a> page. Dane and Rock county owners can start with our <a href="/local-appraisers/madison/">Madison</a> or <a href="/local-appraisers/janesville/">Janesville</a> pages.</p>

<h2>Common questions</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>The adjuster found only a few hits per slope. Can appraisal change that?</summary><div class="a"><p>If the panel finds more damage than the carrier documented, the amount of loss can reflect that. Independent, slope-by-slope documentation is what gives an appraiser something to work with.</p></div></details><details><summary>Will appraisal get me a whole new roof?</summary><div class="a"><p>Not automatically. The panel sets the amount of loss based on the damage and the policy’s terms, and that may or may not support full replacement.</p></div></details><details><summary>My policy pays roofs at actual cash value only. Is appraisal still useful?</summary><div class="a"><p>It can be, because the disagreement over scope and price still matters. The award will just apply the actual cash value terms your policy uses.</p></div></details></div>
